---
description: Mandatory architecture and provenance rules for the x86WASM emulator
alwaysApply: true
---

# Authority

- Intel manuals and approved entries in `docs/sources.md` are authoritative.
- Never invent x86, paging, APIC, ACPI, or device behavior from memory.
- Cite the relevant specification section in tests or a short implementation note.
- Do not copy implementation code from v86, QEMU, Bochs, VirtualBox, DOSBox, or other emulators. Specs and differential oracles only.

# Architecture

- GPRs and architectural addresses use 64-bit-capable types from the first commit.
- Interpreter is the semantic reference; JIT must match its architectural state.
- No browser APIs in `x86-core`, `x86-decode`, `x86-mmu`, `x86-ir`, `x86-interpreter`, or `devices`.
- Never hand-edit generated decoder/table files; change metadata and regenerate.
- CPUID must never expose an unimplemented feature.
- Prefer shared reviewed helpers over duplicated flag/exception semantics.

# Scope

- Implement only the assigned slice. No opportunistic opcodes, devices, or refactors.
- Record unsupported cases explicitly; do not hide them behind advertised features.
